## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to combine shortest-path computation, path counting, and path reconstruction into a cohesive solution. It also evaluates handling of multiple equal-cost routes and lexicographic tie-breaking.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra's algorithm for O(m log n) shortest paths
- Shortest-path DAG construction
- Topological ordering by distance values
- DP for counting distinct shortest paths
- Lexicographic path reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestStats(n, edges, s, t) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    adj[u].push([v, w]);
  }

  const dist = new Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  const pq = [[0, s]];
  dist[s] = 0;

  while (pq.length) {
    const [d, u] = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        pq.push([dist[v], v]);
      }
    }
  }

  const dag = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (let u = 0; u < n; u++) {
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w === dist[v]) {
        dag[u].push(v);
      }
    }
  }

  const ways = new Array(n).fill(0);
  ways[s] = 1;
  const order = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i)
    .sort((a, b) => dist[a] - dist[b]);

  for (const u of order) {
    for (const v of dag[u]) {
      ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % 1000000007;
    }
  }

  const path = [];
  let curr = t;
  while (curr !== null) {
    path.unshift(curr);
    const preds = dag
      .map((list, u) => list.includes(curr) ? u : -1)
      .filter(u => u >= 0 && dist[curr] === dist[u] + 
        adj[u].find(([v]) => v === curr)?.[1]);
    preds.sort((a, b) => a - b);
    curr = preds.length > 0 ? preds[0] : null;
    if (curr === s) {
      path.unshift(s);
      break;
    }
  }

  return { distance: dist[t], ways: ways[t], path };
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- Dijkstra: O(m log n)
- DAG construction: O(m)
- DP counting: O(m)
- Path reconstruction: O(n + m)
- Overall: O(m log n)

## Why This Is Asked

This tests practical graph reasoning: counting shortest paths under a Dijkstra framework, handling path multiplicities without overflow, and edge-case ties.

## Key Concepts

- Dijkstra with path counting
- Tie handling on relaxation
- Complexity and edge-case handling

## Code Example

```javascript
function shortestPaths(graph, s, t) {
  const N = graph.length;
  const dist = Array(N).fill(Infinity);
  const ways = Array(N).fill(0);
  dist[s] = 0;
  ways[s] = 1;
  const pq = new MinHeap((a,b)=>a.d-b.d);
  pq.push({node:s, d:0});
  while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
     const {node:u, d} = pq.pop();
     if (d !== dist[u]) continue;
     for (const [v, w] of graph[u]) {
        const nd = dist[u] + w;
        if (nd < dist[v]) { dist[v] = nd; ways[v] = ways[u]; pq.push({node:v, d:nd}); }
        else if (nd === dist[v]) { ways[v] += ways[u]; }
     }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t], count: ways[t] };
}
```

