## Why This Is Asked
This question tests fundamental graph algorithms, DAG analysis, and dynamic programming techniques commonly used in project scheduling and dependency management systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Topological Sorting**: Linear ordering respecting task dependencies
- **Dynamic Programming on DAGs**: Computing optimal values through predecessor relationships
- **Earliest Finish Times**: Maximum of predecessor completion plus task duration
- **Makespan Calculation**: Overall project completion time
- **Complexity**: O(N + E) time and space

## Code Example
```javascript
function makespan(n, durations, edges) {
  // Build adjacency list and compute indegrees
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v] of edges) {
    adj[u].push(v);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  // Initialize earliest finish times
  const earliestFinish = durations.slice();
  const queue = [];

  // Start with source nodes (indegree = 0)
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) queue.push(i);
  }

  // Process nodes in topological order
  let head = 0;
  while (head < queue.length) {
    const u = queue[head++];

    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      // Update earliest finish for successor
      earliestFinish[v] = Math.max(
        earliestFinish[v],
        earliestFinish[u] + durations[v]
      );

      indeg[v]--;
      if (indeg[v] === 0) queue.push(v);
    }
  }

  // Return makespan (maximum earliest finish time)
  return Math.max(...earliestFinish);
}
```

## Algorithm Insight
The topological approach ensures each node is processed only after all its predecessors are complete, allowing accurate computation of earliest start times and efficient makespan calculation.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a clean DP formulation for segmentation with per-placement costs and wildcards. It also probes backtracking and memoization versus tabulation choices under a dictionary constraint.

## Key Concepts
- DP on string positions with a dictionary
- Memoization vs bottom-up tabulation
- Backtracking reconstruction of segmentation
- Handling wildcards and per-placement costs

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCost(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(null);
  const choice = new Array(n).fill(null);

  function costAt(i, w) {
    let cost = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < w.length; j++) {
      if (s[i+j] !== '?' && s[i+j] !== w[j]) {
        cost++;
      }
    }
    return cost;
  }

  function dp(i) {
    if (i === n) return 0;
    if (memo[i] !== null) return memo[i];

    let best = Infinity;
    let bestWord = null;

    for (const w of dict) {
      if (i + w.length <= n) {
        const c = costAt(i, w) + dp(i + w.length);
        if (c < best) {
          best = c;
          bestWord = w;
        }
      }
    }

    memo[i] = best;
    choice[i] = bestWord;
    return best;
  }

  const minCost = dp(0);
  if (minCost === Infinity) return -1;

  // Reconstruct segmentation
  const words = [];
  let i = 0;
  while (i < n) {
    words.push(choice[i]);
    i += choice[i].length;
  }

  return { cost: minCost, words };
}
```

## Complexity
Time: O(n × sum|w|) where sum|w| is total length of all dictionary words
Space: O(n) for memoization and choice arrays

## Why This Is Asked

